// Practice quiz: Reading: SQL vs NoSQL
// Unreviewed first draft, 2026-11-04. Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.

$CATEGORY: containers-cicd/microservices-and-ci-cd/sql-vs-nosql

::o1 schema::How does a document store typically differ from a relational table on schema? {
=Documents can have different fields without a table-wide change#Correct. Per-document flexibility is the document model.
~Documents forbid nested values#Not quite. Nested values are common.
~Document stores need every field declared up front#Not quite. That describes relational tables.
~Relational tables have no schema#Not quite. They have a declared schema.
}

::o1 query::What does a typical key-value store support well? {
~Multi-table joins#Not quite. That is a relational strength.
=Get and put by key#Correct. Most support only simple operations.
~Ad hoc reporting across fields#Not quite. The value is opaque to most stores.
~Declared foreign keys#Not quite. That is a relational constraint.
}

::o1 consistency::Which store type is known for transactions across many rows with strong constraints? {
~Key-value#Not quite. Its operations are usually simple single-key ones.
~A static file#Not quite. Files have no transactions.
=Relational#Correct. ACID transactions and constraints are its core strength.
~A log aggregator#Not quite. It is not a transactional store.
}

::o2 pattern::A bank ledger must move money between accounts all-or-nothing and run joined reports. Which store fits? {
~Key-value#Not quite. Multi-record all-or-nothing writes are not its strength.
~Document, since JSON is modern#Not quite. Fashion is not an access pattern.
~An in-memory cache alone#Not quite. A cache is not a durable ledger.
=Relational#Correct. Multi-row transactions and joins drive this choice.
}

::o2 justify::Which is the strongest justification for a store choice? {
=How the service reads and writes its data#Correct. Access patterns drive the decision.
~Which product is newest#Not quite. New does not mean suitable.
~Which one the team saw in a conference talk#Not quite. That says nothing about this data.
~The number of stars its repository has#Not quite. Popularity says nothing about this data.
}

::o3 polyglot::What lets a search service use one store type and a billing service another? {
~Sharing one schema#Not quite. One shared database has one store type.
=Each service owning its database#Correct. Private data stores make polyglot persistence possible.
~Using the same ORM#Not quite. An ORM does not change the store.
~Running both on one server#Not quite. Hosting is unrelated.
}
